speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, on November 17 last I asked the Minister of Regional Economic Expansion (Mr. Lessard) whether he had been contacted by the provincial government of Newfoundland for assistance because of the worsening situation at Labrador linerboard mill in my district at Stephenville, Newfoundland. The continuing deterioration of the mill was the result of the fact that the bulk of the resource had to be transported from Labrador, a distance of 600 miles. It must be realized that the resulting cost of producing a ton of linerboard is seriously affected by the transportation cost. Added to this handicap, the distance of the mill from the off-shore market further exacerbates the problem when the mill has to face competition from similar operations closer to the market. This doubles the problem which is difficult to overcome.
Furthermore, adding to the problem is the fact that this mill came onstream at an inopportune time when demand for the product was at a low ebb. Efforts by the sales forces to break into the market left much to be desired. Third, to inflict a further scar on the disease, the wood source near the mill is not available to the mill in enough quantity to relieve the cost of producing a ton of linerboard.
The continuing failure to overcome these problems has resulted in the provincial government of Newfoundland, which owns the mill, having to subsidize the operation at an annual expenditure of some $25 million to protect, in its own interest, and the many hundreds of jobs directly and indirectly related.
It has now reached the point where new management has been brought in, and an advisory board with economic expertise has been appointed to determine the future viability of the operation, subject to the consideration of various options, and it is my opinion that with federal government assistance the mill and its operation can be rehabilitated, to the benefit of a depressed community suffering from the continuing downturn of economic events.
Having reviewed the problem with the assistance of officials in the federal departments concerned, and after making my own assessment, I came to some conclusions, and they are best expressed in a letter I wrote to the Secretary of State for External Affairs (Mr. Jamieson). He indicated his concern through his own portfolio and when he was minister of industry, trade and commerce. In my letter of November 8, 1976,1

February 3, 1977
Adjournment Debate
expressed the problems as I saw them and as they relate to the mill, and other factors, and I recommended the following:
1) That the federal government initiate a co-ordinated effort with the provincial government under and including the Department of Industry, Trade and Commerce, the Department of Regional Economic Expansion, the Department of Manpower, along with probably Cl DA and the Export Development Corporation directed towards the following objectives:
a)	to review studies carried out on the operation of the mill;
b)	to examine ways and means of rationalizing the cost of the resource in line with availability;
c)	to examine the possibility and the need for support funding to streamline the operation in order to compete with offshore capacity;
d)	to determine the need for assistance in shipping cost to and from the mill as a result of distance from the resource as well as the distance from offshore markets;
e)	to consider in the determination of assistance to underdeveloped countries to whom Canada provides interest free loans to establish mills, the feasibility of promoting the purchase of linerboard on the same basis (ClDA);
0 in the continuing efforts to establish trade with other countries that the sale of linerboard be given high priority;
g)	to examine the possibility of finding firms that use linerboard extensively to invest in Labrador Linerboard in order to ensure capacity where those firms would provide a steady market, or alternatively to consider through Export Development Corporation, the possibility of investing in firms that use an abundance of linerboard to ensure sales, or to invest in the mill itself;
h)	to consult with all firms producing linerboard, that may have plans for expansion in line with expanding markets to invest or purchase the Labrador Linerboard Mill;
i)	that Manpower extend efforts to examine the results of high unemployment that is occurring and to examine and implement re-training and other programs, to prepare for the impending need for mobility transfers and alternative job opportunities;
j)	that the facilities at the Harmon Base be examined to recognize the possibility of considering that base in the government's decentralization objectives, e.g. Taxation Data Centre, Canadian Coast Guard (Search and Rescue) fisheries, etc.;
k)	to investigate the physical inventory of valuable property left as a result of the phase out of the American SEAC Base and to determine its use as a result of decentralization planning.
In recent days I have discussed with the mill management the present status of the mill, and I have confirmed that immediate co-operative action by all concerned is necessary. I am convinced that with the evident upturn in the whole pulp and paper industry, the operation can succeed. I am confident that the present management is capable of revitalizing the mill. It has a new dedication and determination, not just with regard to the mill itself but with regard to saving the economy of an important sector of the province.
I can only appeal to the Minister of Regional Economic Expansion to make a determined effort to co-ordinate federal support with his colleagues concerned to form a team and to meet with mill management and Newfoundland provincial officials with a realistic and positive approach to ensure the success of the operation, and with a flexible approach to ensure the protection of an economic base for one sector of our nation.
